Commentary of al-Biqa'i (Nazm al-Durar) on surah 7, verse 19:

When He had made obligatory for him the wretchedness He mentioned, namely his persistence in envy and his excessive speech about the one he envied, He turned to the one he envied, about whom he had not spoken a single word, but rather occupied himself with weeping over his own sin and was content with what his Lord did to save him from the snares of his deception, which he had set up through what was mentioned, so that this would be the cause of his happiness. So He said, coordinating with ﴿Get out of it﴾ [al-A'raf: 18]: ﴿And you, Adam, dwell﴾. And since the intended meaning of this command is the person himself, and not a metaphorical reference to some of those associated with him, He emphasized the pronoun to confirm the coordination and remove any figurative reading, so it was said: ﴿You and your wife, dwell in the Garden﴾.

Since the context here is to make known that He established for our father in the Garden a greater establishment than He gave us on earth, by granting him in it abundant living, coinciding with his existence; then the conjunction with the fa' (ﻓ), which indicates that the eating occurred together with the dwelling and did not come after it, is appropriate in His saying ﴿So eat﴾ (fa-kulā). There is no contradiction between this and the expression with the waw in al-Baqarah, because the concept of the fa' is a type included under the concept of the waw, and there is no contradiction between a species and a genus. His saying ﴿from wherever you will﴾ (min ḥaythu shi'tumā) carries the meaning of ﴿raghadan﴾, i.e., abundantly, for it indicates the permission to eat from everything in it except what was forbidden. As for the verse in al-Baqarah, it indicates permission to eat from it in whatever place it may be. This context, to its end, points out that whoever opposes the command of God, exalted is He, has his throne torn down and his might destroyed, even if he is at the height of power and the peak of strength, just as He expelled the one to whom He had granted the greatest power by making the angels prostrate to him, establishing him in His Garden, and permitting everything in it except one tree. He emphasized its prohibition by forbidding approach to it, rather than merely forbidding its enjoyment, so He said: ﴿And do not approach﴾, i.e., much less that you partake of ﴿this tree﴾, indicating a specific tree or its kind. Then He made disobedience a consequence of approaching, for whoever circles around the protected zone is about to fall into it, so He said: ﴿lest you become﴾, i.e., by means of approaching it, ﴿of the wrongdoers﴾, i.e., by eating from it, which is the purpose of the prohibition, so that by that you would be doing the deed of one who walks in darkness;
